  surprise later — with no test runner in the repo, the session module ships unverified except by manual check in a
  browser. That's your call and I'll follow it; I'm noting it because the storage-fallback path is exactly the kind of
  thing a test would catch and a manual click-through won't.

  Here's the design. [1mSection 1 of 2[0m — architecture and data flow.

  [1mArchitecture.[0m One new file, [38;5;153msession.js[39m, loaded by [38;5;153m<script src="session.js">[39m immediately before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m in every page
  that needs the id. It defines a single global, [38;5;153mSession[39m, and nothing else. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m keeps its current shape: globals, no
  build step, no module syntax.

  [38;5;153mlogin[39m does not gain a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter. Its signature stays [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m. What changes is its return
  value: [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m becomes [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username, userId }[39m. Today [38;5;153mlogin[39m is a stub, so
  it will return a placeholder id and a comment marking it as the value the real API response will supply — I'd rather
  have one obviously-fake constant than invent an id-generation scheme the server will overwrite.

  [1mData flow.[0m Form submit → [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m (unchanged) → [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m → the submit handler calls
  [38;5;153mSession.set(result.userId)[39m when [38;5;153msuccess[39m is true → any later page loads [38;5;153msession.js[39m and calls [38;5;153mSession.get()[39m. Reads and
  writes go through [38;5;153mSession[39m; nothing else touches [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m directly, so the storage mechanism stays a one-line
  change inside the module if you later want [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m or a server round trip.

  [1mInterface.[0m Three functions, deliberately small:

  - [38;5;153mSession.set(userId)[39m — stores the id. Ignores [38;5;153mnull[39m/[38;5;153mundefined[39m rather than writing the string [38;5;153m"undefined"[39m, which is the
    classic [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m footgun.
  - [38;5;153mSession.get()[39m — returns the id, or [38;5;153mnull[39m if nothing is stored.
  - [38;5;153mSession.clear()[39m — removes it. Needed for logout; there's no logout UI today, so this ships unused but is part of the
    interface because adding it later means touching every consumer.

  [1mWhat I am not building:[0m no logout button, no UI that displays the id, no changes to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m's form, and nothing in
  [38;5;153msrc/[39m (that CommonJS [38;5;153mgreet[39m entry point is unrelated to the browser app and I'll leave it alone).

  Does this look right so far? If yes, I'll give you section 2 — error handling, the exact files touched, and how we
  verify it without a test runner.

